/**

@page toolbox_blockstructured Blockstructured

\tableofcontents

The block-structured toolbox (`toolbox::blockstructured`, residing in
`src/toolbox/blockstructured`) collects the routines and algorithms that
operate on patch data, i.e. on small (topologically) Cartesian meshes that
are embedded into the cells of Peano's adaptive Cartesian grid. A patch holds
a @f$ k \times k @f$ or @f$ k \times k \times k @f$ block of degrees of
freedom, plus an optional halo layer around it. The toolbox is not tied to a
particular numerical scheme; it provides the building blocks that extensions
such as ExaHyPE 2 (and the finite-volume and Runge-Kutta solvers in
particular) assemble into a solver.


## Functionality

The toolbox is a collection of free functions grouped into a handful of
headers:

\li `Enumeration.h` Lexicographic serialisation of voxel and patch indices,
  both within a patch and within the @f$ 3^{d} @f$ / @f$ 3^{d-1} @f$ patch
  neighbourhoods that arise around faces. These routines define the canonical
  ordering of all patch data.
\li `Copy.h` Copy individual unknowns from one patch into another patch of the
  same resolution. Used to shuffle data between fields that share a layout but
  hold different quantities.
\li `Projection.h` Project patch data onto the adjacent faces
  (`projectPatchSolutionOntoFaces`). The more elaborate time-stepping schemes
  generate their own projection code via the Python API; the C++ routines here
  serve the simpler cases and user pre-processing.
\li `Interpolation.h` Interpolation from a coarse patch onto an embedded fine
  patch at resolution transitions (halo handling for AMR). Several schemes are
  available, including piecewise-constant, linear and tensor-product variants.
\li `Restriction.h` The dual operation: restriction of fine-grid data back
  onto the coarse patch. Injection, averaging and the various tensor-product /
  higher-order schemes are implemented here.
\li `Derivative.h` Computation of derivatives across a patch.
\li `GlobalDatabase.h` A global database that aggregates per-patch scalar
  quantities (e.g. for reductions or global diagnostics) and dumps them to
  file.

To plot patch data, use the writers in `tarch::plotter` (the VTU, HDF5 and
NetCDF writers together with `tarch::plotter::TimeSeriesWriter` for the
snapshot/index handling) rather than any toolbox routine.

*/
